      <div class="mt-10 space-y-3">
        {[
          { q: 'How much does it cost to run a motel?', a: 'It depends on room count, staffing model, and utilities, but the biggest controllables are wages, commissions, and maintenance debt. A system reduces variation so costs stop creeping upward silently.' },
          { q: 'What is the best motel management software?', a: 'The best PMS is the one your team uses correctly. Focus on clean data, consistent workflows, and reporting discipline before switching platforms.' },
          { q: 'How do I run a motel without being on the front desk every day?', a: 'Document standards, train to checklists, and build a weekly management rhythm. Autonomy is earned by systems—never by hope.' },
          { q: 'How do I find a reliable motel manager?', a: 'Hire for discipline and consistency, then support them with a playbook. A strong manager will still fail in a weak system.' },
          { q: 'How do I reduce Booking.com dependency?', a: 'Fix parity, strengthen direct conversion, and run OTAs as billboards. The goal is channel mix control, not channel elimination.' },
          { q: 'What are the core motel management systems I need first?', a: 'Housekeeping standards, cash and rate integrity, distribution checks, maintenance triage, and review velocity. Start with the engine room.' },
          { q: 'How do I train front desk staff quickly?', a: 'Use scripts, checklists, and real scenarios. Reduce variation in guest handling and daily admin so errors stop recurring.' },
          { q: 'How often should I change room rates?', a: 'Weekly is a minimum. In high-demand markets you can review more frequently, but rules matter more than frequency.' },
          { q: 'How do I improve motel reviews?', a: 'Set standards, reduce room defects, and implement a simple review request workflow. Reviews are a system output.' },
          { q: 'How do I run a small motel business profitably?', a: 'Protect margin first: wage discipline, parity control, defect reduction, and direct conversion. Small motels win by consistency, not complexity.' },
          { q: 'Do you need experience to be a motel manager?', a: 'Not always. Many motel owners value attitude, reliability, and willingness to learn over formal management experience.' },
          { q: 'Is motel management suitable for beginners?', a: 'Yes, in many cases it is. Many motel managers start with little or no direct motel experience. Owners often look for people who are reliable, organised, good with guests, and willing to learn on the job. Smaller motels in regional areas are often the most beginner-friendly, as they provide hands-on experience across all parts of the business.' },
          { q: 'Is accommodation included in a motel manager role?', a: 'Most motel manager roles include on-site accommodation, particularly in regional locations. This varies by property and should always be confirmed before accepting a role.' },
          { q: 'What skills should I focus on before becoming a motel manager?', a: 'Depending on your current background and previous work experience, we would suggest looking at learning the computer system, reading our book The Essential Guide to Motel Management, and considering a short role on reception in a local property — which will give you a good starting foundation.' },
        ].map((item) => (
